From: EdRegis@aol.com
Date: Thu, 24 Nov 1994 10:27:31 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-356] NANOTECH: Drexler on self-replication 

Gregory Sullivan (Thu, Nov 24, 1994 2:33 AM EST) writes:
>I think Ed Regis is correct when he states that much 
>can be accomplished in nanotechnology without
>self-replication.  The description he gives above
>corresponds to what Drexler calls Stage One 
>systems I believe.  However, in Nanosystems Drexler 
>calls replication ``a key threshold in development''.
>It is used to achieve Stage Two and Three systems.

He does call it a key threshold, and he does make this comment in the context
of building later-generation systems, but this doesn't mean that
self-replication is *requirement* for those systems.  (While he describes
what happens *if* a system reproduces itself, he nevertheless does not say
that self-replication is a necessary stage or element for the development of
later systems.)  A system's copying itself is a key threshold rather in the
sense of being a marker of the degree of complexity achieved; also it's a
nice capability to have, obviously, and it certainly can be used to get to
later systems.  Still, it's not *necessary* in order to get to them.  

(I myself don't see anything particularly challenging about self-replication,
since if a molecular manufactuing unit can build systems of any arbitrary
complexity, then building another system exactly like itself is just a
special case of whatever else it can build.  Everything hinges on whether the
machine's program is sufficient to the task.  (The problem of
self-referential blueprints can be solved in the same way the ribosomes do
it, by reading them once as instructions, and copying them once as a
program.))    

FYI, an extract from a for-the-record Q&A I did with Drexler on 3/11/92
(while he was still writing Nanosystems), anent the subject of the necessity
of self-replication for the development of nanotechnology.  

QUOTE from Drexler 3/11/92:  

"Another change [from my earlier conception of nanotechnology] at the systems
level has been an increasing appreciation for the fact that virtually all of
the advantages that one might see in self-replicating machines can be
obtained without building a self-replicating machine, if by a
self-replicating machine you mean something that takes simple raw materials
and makes a copy of itself based on its internal information, something like
genes.  The developments that I am currently exploring that are described in
the new book involve mechanisms building other mechanisms like themselves but
in response to a steady stream of instructions from the outside, and that's a
big advantage because it means the mechanism is simpler.  It doesn't have an
on-board computer.  It doesn't have an on-board information storage system
and so it's a much better place to start than attempting to build a genuine
self-replicating machine.  And in the longer term you have large systems, and
whether one builds something that can build a copy of itself at that point is
quite optional."  

END quote from Drexler 3/11/92.

